      "text": " A) The company will ensure that it can offer its employees any training necessary for meeting their job requirements.\n<think>\nTo determine the best plan for preparing a company to effectively handle the rapid obsolescence of skills, let's analyze each option step by step:\n\nA) Ensuring that the company can offer any training necessary for meeting job requirements: This option suggests that the company should be prepared to provide ongoing training to its employees to keep them up-to-date with the latest advancements in AMT. This approach acknowledges the need for continuous learning and adaptation in the workplace. By offering training, the company can ensure that its employees remain relevant and capable of performing their jobs effectively.\n\nB) Developing a program to offer selected employees the opportunity to receive training six years after they were originally hired: This option implies that the company will only provide training to a select group of employees after they have been with the company for six years. This approach may not be sufficient to address the rapid obsolescence of skills, as it does not account for the fact that skills become obsolete within as little as five years. Additionally, it may not be practical to identify and train only a few employees after they have been with the company for six years.\n\nC) Increasing investment in AMT every year for a period of at least five years: This option suggests that the company should invest in AMT to stay ahead of the curve and ensure that it remains competitive. While this approach may help the company stay relevant in the industry, it does not directly address the need for training to keep employees up-to-date with the latest advancements in AMT. Moreover, it may not be feasible for all companies to invest in AMT every year for five years.\n\nD) Periodically surveying employees to determine how the introduction of AMT has affected them: This option suggests that the company should periodically assess the impact of AMT on its employees. While this approach may provide valuable insights into the challenges and opportunities presented by AMT, it does not directly address the need for training to keep employees up-to-date with the latest advancements in AMT. Additionally, it may not be practical to conduct periodic surveys to determine the impact of AMT on employees.\n\nBased on the analysis, option A) The company will ensure that it can offer its employees any training necessary for meeting their job requirements, appears to be the most effective plan for preparing the company to effectively handle the rapid obsolescence of skills described above. This option acknowledges the need for continuous learning and adaptation in the workplace, ensuring that employees remain relevant and capable of performing their jobs effectively.\n</think>\n<answer>\nA) The company will ensure that it can offer its employees any training necessary for meeting their job requirements.\n</answer>",
