  "choices": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "text": " D) In 2009, China's rare earth reserves accounted for 36% of the national reserves, the United States accounted for 13%, Russia accounted for 19%, Australia accounted for 5.4%; China's production accounted for 97% of the world's production, and the other three countries were all zero.\n<think>\nTo refute the countries' accusations of China monopolizing rare earth resources, we need to provide evidence that shows China's control over the rare earth industry is not as significant as they claim. Let's analyze each option step by step:\n\nA) Rare earths are non-renewable and important strategic resources.\nThis statement is true, but it doesn't directly address China's control over the rare earth industry. It only highlights the importance of rare earths.\n\nB) At present, on a per capita basis, China is already a country with relatively scarce rare earth resources.\nThis statement is also true, but it doesn't provide any information about China's control over the rare earth industry.\n\nC) Since the 1980s, rare earths have been exploited indiscriminately in some parts of China, causing serious waste of resources and great damage to the environment.\nThis statement is true, but it doesn't directly address China's control over the rare earth industry.\n\nD) In 2009, China's rare earth reserves accounted for 36% of the national reserves, the United States accounted for 13%, Russia accounted for 19%, Australia accounted for 5.4%; China's production accounted for 97% of the world's production, and the other three countries were all zero.\nThis statement provides evidence that China's control over the rare earth industry is not as significant as the countries accuse it to be. It shows that China's reserves and production account for a large portion of the world's total, but the other countries still have significant reserves and production as well. This statement directly refutes the countries' accusations of China monopolizing rare earth resources.\n</think>\n<answer>\nD) In 2009, China's rare earth reserves accounted for 36% of the national reserves, the United States accounted for 13%, Russia accounted for 19%, Australia accounted for 5.4%; China's production accounted for 97% of the world's production, and the other three countries were all zero.\n</answer>",
